assert — Stop if a condition is false                                        core · tier 0 · since 0.5.0 · 1.0 · exact

USAGE
    assert(condition, message)

DESCRIPTION
    Checks a result and stops the script with `message` when the check fails: the error is Z0001, the code
    of `error`, and the exit code is 1 (section 3.8 of the Language Reference). When the check holds,
    nothing is printed and the script carries on, so an `assert` costs nothing to read and guards a re-run:
    a row count after a filter, a share between 0 and 1, a coefficient with the expected sign.
    `assert(equal(f(x), [1, 2]), "...")` is how a project tests its own functions.

    The condition is one Bool. `NA` is neither true nor false and is an error (Z0203), as it is for `if`, so
    a check on a value that may be missing tests `is_missing` first; a column is not one Bool either
    (Z0203): reduce it with `all` or `any`, as in `assert(all(d["x"] > 0), ...)`. Like R's `stopifnot`, with
    a message of your own; like Stata's `assert`, which stops with "assertion is false".

ARGUMENTS
    condition   Bool. The check, a Bool.
    message     Str. What to say when it fails.

VALUE
    Nothing.

MISSING VALUES
    A condition that is `NA` is neither true nor false and stops the script (Z0203): a check on a value that
    may be missing tests `is_missing` first.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    Z0203   the condition is `NA`
    Z0001   the script stopped itself with the message given

A COMMON MISTAKE
    Giving a comparison of a whole column. That is one answer for each row, not one Bool; check every row
    with `all`, as in `assert(all(d["x"] > 0), "x is positive")`. The message says the same:

    > assert(d["x"] > 0, "x is positive")
    error[Z0203]: `assert` needs one Bool, but this is a Column<Bool>
      = help: reduce the column to one Bool: `assert(all(d["x"] > 0), ...)` checks every row, `any` checks for one

COMING FROM R OR STATA
    Equivalents: R stopifnot(); Stata assert.

    Zeff                                    R                                           Stata
    assert(n == 3, "three rows expected")   stopifnot("three rows expected" = n == 3)   assert _N == 3

    Stata's `assert` checks its condition in every row of the data and has no message of its own; here the
    condition is one Bool, and `all` makes one of a column. R's `stopifnot` takes several conditions at
    once; here each has its own `assert` and its own message.

RELATED TASKS
    - To stop without a condition, use `error`.
    - To compare two values that may be lists or records, use `equal`.

ACCURACY
    Exact.

SEE ALSO
    error, warn, equal, all

EXAMPLES
    // A check that holds prints nothing, and the script carries on.
    > assert(1 + 1 == 2, "arithmetic still holds")
    > let d = frame(x = [1, 2, 3])
    > assert(d.n_rows() == 3, "three rows expected")
    > print("both checks held")
    both checks held

    // A check that fails stops the script with its message.
    > assert(d.n_rows() == 4, "four rows expected")
    error[Z0001]: four rows expected
     --> examples.zeff:5:1
      |
    5 | assert(d.n_rows() == 4, "four rows expected")
      | ^^^^^^ in this call
